PCGrad shifts cosine similarity to oscillate around zero, yet TF metrics are unchanged. AR GAR \emph{worsens}: 0.064$\to$0.030 ($p$\,=\,0.002; Figure~\ref{fig:training-pcgrad}b in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-gradient}). By \emph{modus tollens}: removing gradient conflict does not prevent collapse; therefore it is not the cause. The 7.5--14.7$\times$ magnitude dominance renders gradient conflict inconsequential. Cross-model analysis confirms this pattern: Llama-3.1-8B gradients are near-orthogonal (cos\,=\,+0.005) with 25.7$\times$ NTP dominance (Table~\ref{tab:gradient-crossmodel}).

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_ss_multiseed.pdf}
  \caption{Multi-seed AR GAR under scheduled sampling ($\beta$\,=\,0 and $\beta$\,=\,5, three seeds). Steering nearly eliminates seed variance (std: 0.047$\to$0.001).}
  \label{fig:ss-multiseed}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Distribution Shift Analysis}\label{subsec:dist-shift}

The TF-AR GAR gap persists across all variants (Table~\ref{tab:four-way}). GAR-Dongre provides a control: it remains uniformly low (0.01--0.02) with no distribution gap, confirming that the shift selectively destroys concentrated last-token attention. The mechanism is exposure bias \citep{bengio2015scheduled,zhang2019bridging,arora2022exposure}: during training the model conditions on ground-truth prefixes; at inference, on its own outputs. GAR optimizes a distributional property with no autoregressive counterpart, explaining why TF GAR-ours remains near $\tau$\,=\,0.5 while AR GAR collapses (77--87\% per-turn decay; Figure~\ref{fig:attention-heatmap}; scaling analysis in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-eb-scaling}).

\subsection{Mechanism Specificity}\label{subsec:mechanism-specificity}

We test specificity with Turn-level Residual Coherence (TRC), the cosine similarity between adjacent-turn hidden states. TF/AR TRC is 0.334/0.332 for ThreadGate (gap $+$0.002) and 0.349/0.411 for LoRA-only (gap $-$0.062, reversed), confirming exposure bias disrupts a specific routing behavior while leaving representations intact. Cross-model evaluation confirms: GAR-Dongre shows negligible shift on both Llama (1.002$\times$) and Qwen (1.28$\times$) vs.\ the order-of-magnitude GAR-ours collapse.

\subsection{Cross-Model Replication}\label{subsec:cross-model}

We replicate on Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct \citep{grattafiori2024llama3} (Base, ThreadGate, LoRA-only; $N$\,=\,20, 4 turns). TF GAR-ours is 0.484 (TG) / 0.481 (LoRA-only); gating contributes zero benefit. The AR distribution is heavy-tailed (coefficient of variation, CoV\,$>$\,100\%); we report medians: 0.018 (TG and LoRA-only), 0.009 (Base), yielding gaps of 27$\times$ and 26$\times$ (Figure~\ref{fig:crossmodel}a in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-llama}). Gate profiles differ qualitatively (Figure~\ref{fig:crossmodel}b): Qwen attenuates early layers (L1\,=\,0.793) while Llama dips at late layers (L25\,=\,0.719), an architecture-dependent pattern, but convergence toward identity is universal (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-viz}). The gap is substantial on both architectures (8--17$\times$ on Qwen, 27$\times$ on Llama), consistent with exposure bias as architecture-independent.

\enlargethispage{2\baselineskip}
Scheduled sampling confirms cross-model generalization: Llama SS compresses the gap from ${\sim}$27$\times$ to 2.9$\times$ (TF GAR 0.477, AR GAR 0.166), weaker than Qwen's ${\sim}$1.3$\times$ likely due to fewer training epochs (2 vs.\ 3). The phase transition is architecture-dependent: $\beta$\,=\,3 triggers recovery on Qwen (0.556) but not Llama (0.173); Llama requires $\beta$\,=\,5 (GAR 0.791, exceeding Qwen SS 0.717), suggesting deeper architectures need higher steering thresholds but ultimately provide more surface for attention reweighting (full results in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-llama}). Cross-scale validation on Qwen-2.5-1.5B confirms the same pattern: 79.8\% GAR decay and $\beta$-steering recovery to 0.715 (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-1.5b}).

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_gate_shift.pdf}
  \caption{(a)~Gate turn conditioning: per-layer gate values at turn~1 vs turn~21. In 27/28 layers, gates approach 1.0 at late turns, inverting the design intent ($p = 3.73 \times 10^{-8}$). (b)~Distribution shift ($N$\,=\,5 pilot): the TF-AR GAR gap persists across all five variants. Pilot gaps (20--34$\times$) exceed final $N$\,=\,50 estimates (8--17$\times$; Table~\ref{tab:four-way}) due to smaller sample size.}
  \label{fig:gate-shift}
\end{figure}

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Setup}

We fine-tune Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct with LoRA \citep{hu2022lora,dettmers2023qlora} on 8K multi-turn conversations from the UltraChat \citep{ding2023ultrachat} training split. Training uses 3 epochs with AdamW (lr $= 2 \times 10^{-4}$, batch size 1, gradient accumulation 8, seed 42; ThreadGate additionally with seed 123); each variant requires approximately 6 hours on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition GPU (96\,GB; training costs in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-cost}). Full hyperparameters are in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-hyper}; dataset statistics in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-data}; the controlled comparison design is described in \S\ref{sec:comparison-design}. We evaluate under \emph{IID} (same-distribution UltraChat text) and \emph{held-out} (50 non-overlapping conversations) teacher-forced protocols, reporting GAR-ours, GAR-Dongre \citep{dongre2026attention}, and perplexity (PPL).

\subsection{Main Results}

Table~\ref{tab:four-way} presents the five-way comparison. Under TF evaluation, all fine-tuned variants converge: GAR-ours spans 0.49--0.51 ($\leq$2\,pp), PPL is 2.78 for all. IID and held-out protocols produce identical rankings (held-out GAR-ours differs by $\leq$1\,pp), ruling out data overlap as a confound. GAR-Dongre remains low (0.01--0.02) with no TF-AR gap, confirming that only concentrated last-token attention is vulnerable to distribution shift; scheduled sampling breaks this pattern (\S\ref{subsec:ss}).

Under AR generation ($N$\,=\,50), GAR-ours drops to 0.019--0.064, an 8--17$\times$ gap (Figure~\ref{fig:headline}). The ordering (ThreadGate 0.064 $>$ LoRA-only 0.037 $>$ Uniform Gate 0.033 $>$ PCGrad 0.030 $>$ Base 0.019) shows ThreadGate vs.\ LoRA-only is significant ($p$\,=\,0.004; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-stats}), yet the gap remains 7.8$\times$. A second seed confirms TF stability ($0.504\pm0.001$). MMLU 5-shot accuracy remains within 0.8\,pp across all variants (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-mmlu}). We analyze this distribution shift in \S\ref{sec:analysis}.

\subsection{Inference-Time Attention Steering}\label{subsec:steering}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig5_attention_steering.pdf}
  \caption{Inference-time $\beta$-steering recovers GAR from 0.064 to 0.727, confirming attention misallocation as the proximal cause (9\% PPL cost at $\beta$\,=\,3; phase transition at $\beta \in [2, 3]$).}
  \label{fig:steering-recovery}
\end{figure}

All training-time interventions leave the exposure bias gap intact. If the collapse is caused by attention misallocation rather than representation degradation, then directly correcting attention should recover GAR. We test this by adding a constant bias $\beta$ to pre-softmax attention logits for goal-token positions during generation:
\begin{equation}
  \tilde{A}_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)} = A_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)} + \beta \cdot \mathbf{1}[j \in \mathcal{G}],
\end{equation}
where $\tilde{A}$ denotes the modified logits before softmax. The additive form operates in log-probability space, providing uniform boost even when baseline attention is near zero. The response is sharply nonlinear: $\beta \in [0, 2]$ yields marginal improvement, then $\beta = 3$ triggers a phase transition to GAR\,=\,0.556 (9\% PPL cost). At $\beta$\,=\,5, GAR reaches 0.727$\pm$0.061 (PPL 1.77); the operating region $\beta \in [3, 5]$ exceeds the TF baseline while PPL remains below 1.8 (Figure~\ref{fig:steering-recovery}; full results in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-steering}). The recovery confirms attention misallocation, not representation degradation, as the proximal cause: if the collapse were due to hidden-state drift, attention reweighting could not recover GAR (qualitative examples in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-examples}).

\subsection{Scheduled Sampling Compresses the TF--AR Gap}\label{subsec:ss}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_ss_gap_compression.pdf}
  \caption{Scheduled sampling compresses the TF-AR gap from 7.8$\times$ to ${\sim}$1.3$\times$, the only variant approaching parity across five training conditions.}
  \label{fig:ss-gap}
\end{figure}

Scheduled sampling (SS; \citealp{bengio2015scheduled}), which mixes model-generated tokens into training prefixes with linearly increasing probability (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-ss}), directly targets the training-inference mismatch. Across three seeds, AR GAR-ours rises to $0.401\pm0.047$, compressing the gap from 7.8$\times$ to ${\sim}$1.3$\times$ (TF GAR $0.498\pm0.004$) with no PPL cost ($1.441\pm0.001$; Figure~\ref{fig:ss-gap}; per-seed details in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-perseed}). Steering on SS models nearly eliminates seed variance ($\beta$\,=\,5: $0.717\pm0.001$, std 0.047$\to$0.001; Figure~\ref{fig:ss-multiseed}). SS generalizes across GAR definitions (mid-turn GAR above 0.36 for all token-position variants; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-gar-robust}) and across architectures: on Llama-3.1-8B, SS compresses the gap from ${\sim}$27$\times$ to 2.9$\times$, weaker than Qwen likely due to fewer training epochs (2 vs.\ 3; \S\ref{subsec:cross-model}). The benefit diminishes in longer dialogues (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-n5}), consistent with exposure bias compounding over turns.

\section{Method}\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Preliminaries}\label{sec:background}

\paragraph{Goal Accessibility Ratio.}
Standard metrics such as perplexity and attention entropy capture generation quality but not \emph{what} the model attends to. In multi-turn dialogue, a model may generate fluent text while progressively ignoring task specifications in the system prompt. We need a metric that directly measures whether goal-relevant context receives attention. To formalize this, we denote the task specification tokens in the system prompt as \emph{goal tokens} $\mathcal{G}$. \citet{dongre2026attention} defined the Goal Accessibility Ratio (GAR) for a model with $L$ layers and $H$ heads at turn $\tau$ as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gar}
  \text{GAR}(\tau) = \frac{1}{L \cdot H \cdot |\mathcal{R}_\tau|} \sum_{\ell=1}^{L} \sum_{h=1}^{H} \sum_{i \in \mathcal{R}_\tau} \sum_{j \in \mathcal{G}} A_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)},
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{R}_\tau$ is the set of response tokens at turn $\tau$ and $A_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)}$ is the attention weight from token $i$ to token $j$ at layer $\ell$, head $h$.

\paragraph{Two GAR variants.}
\textbf{GAR-Dongre} is the original definition (Eq.~\ref{eq:gar}), averaging over all response tokens. However, averaging dilutes the signal with intermediate positions that may legitimately attend to local context rather than the system prompt. The last token of each assistant turn is where the model aggregates information for the next continuation---attention at this position most directly reflects the model's awareness of task specifications. \textbf{GAR-ours} restricts the measurement accordingly:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gar-ours}
  \text{GAR\text{-}ours}(\tau) = \frac{1}{L \cdot H} \sum_{\ell=1}^{L} \sum_{h=1}^{H} \sum_{j \in \mathcal{G}} A_{t_\tau,\, j}^{(\ell,h)},
\end{equation}
where $t_\tau$ is the last token index at turn $\tau$. Attention at this position reflects the information drawn upon for continuation; we use GAR-ours as the optimization target for $\mathcal{L}_\text{GAR}$ (\S\ref{sec:loss}). Throughout this work, GAR serves primarily as a \emph{diagnostic probe}: it measures whether auxiliary attention training transfers to autoregressive generation, rather than being an end-to-end optimization objective whose increase directly improves downstream task performance (the correlation with instruction retention is weak; $r$\,=\,0.200, $p$\,=\,0.007; \S\ref{sec:discussion}).

\subsection{ThreadGate Architecture}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig4_attention_heatmap.pdf}
  \caption{Per-turn GAR-ours under AR generation ($N$\,=\,5 conversations, 10 turns). All fine-tuned variants cluster together (77--81\% decay; $\Delta$\,=\,3.3\,pp between ThreadGate and LoRA-only); Base decays 87.5\%. Gating adds zero incremental effect.}
  \label{fig:attention-heatmap}
\end{figure}

Different transformer layers serve different roles: early layers capture local syntax while later layers integrate long-range context \citep{gromov2024unreasonable}. A global intervention would uniformly scale all layers, but goal attention decay is layer-dependent: some layers contribute more to cross-turn information routing than others. ThreadGate therefore adds a \emph{per-layer} gating mechanism to the residual stream, allowing the model to learn which layers to modulate. At each layer $l$, the residual update is scaled by a scalar gate:
\begin{equation}
  x_{l+1} = x_l + g_l \cdot f_l(x_l),
\end{equation}
where $f_l$ denotes the sublayer computation (self-attention or feed-forward network, FFN). Since residual networks can be viewed as ensembles of shallow paths \citep{veit2016residual}, per-layer gating selectively modulates individual paths within this ensemble. This resembles ReZero \citep{bachlechner2021rezero}, which initializes a single trainable scalar at zero to stabilize deep network training, but ThreadGate differs in three ways: (i)~gates are \emph{content-conditioned}, a function of the hidden state rather than a fixed scalar; (ii)~gates are \emph{turn-conditioned}, incorporating conversation-position features; (iii)~gates are initialized near identity ($\sigma(1.5) \approx 0.82$) rather than zero, preserving the pretrained model's computation.

Concretely, each gate $g_l$ is produced by a two-layer MLP that takes both content and turn features as input:
\begin{align}\label{eq:gate}
  g_l = \sigma\bigl(W_2\,\text{ReLU}(&W_1\,[\text{proj}(x_l);\;\text{emb}(t_d, t_i)] \notag\\
  &+ b_1) + b_2\bigr),
\end{align}
where $\text{proj}(\cdot)$ reduces the hidden dimension to 16, and $\text{emb}(t_d, t_i)$ provides a 16-dimensional turn embedding encoding turn distance $t_d$ (turns since system prompt) and turn index $t_i$ via learned lookup tables. The 32-dimensional input passes through a 64-unit hidden layer to a scalar gate in $(0, 1)$ via sigmoid. For Qwen-2.5-7B ($L\!=\!28$), this adds ${\sim}$57K parameters ($<$0.001\% of 7B). To isolate the contribution of turn conditioning, the \textbf{Uniform Gate} ablation replaces $\text{emb}(\cdot)$ with a learnable constant, removing turn awareness while retaining content-conditioned gating. To preserve pretrained behavior, gate biases are initialized to 1.5 (${\sim}$82\% pass-through) rather than zero as in ReZero, and gate parameters receive a $10\times$ learning rate multiplier to accelerate adaptation.

\subsection{Training Objective}\label{sec:loss}

Next-token prediction alone provides no gradient signal for attention allocation; a model can achieve low perplexity while distributing attention arbitrarily. The auxiliary GAR loss provides explicit pressure to maintain goal-token attention, while a diversity regularizer prevents gate collapse. The training objective combines three terms:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_\text{NTP} + \lambda_\text{GAR}\,\mathcal{L}_\text{GAR} + \lambda_\text{div}\,\mathcal{L}_\text{div}.
\end{equation}
$\mathcal{L}_\text{NTP}$ is the standard next-token prediction loss. For $\mathcal{L}_\text{GAR}$, we use MSE rather than cross-entropy because GAR is a continuous ratio and MSE provides \emph{bidirectional} pressure---penalizing both under- and over-attention, preventing identity collapse (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-loss}):
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_\text{GAR} = \text{MSE}(\text{GAR\text{-}ours}, \tau),
\end{equation}
where $\tau = 0.5$ targets allocating half of the last token's attention to goal tokens. Without diversity pressure, all gates converge near 1.0; the gate diversity loss prevents this:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_\text{div} = \text{ReLU}(\sigma_\text{min} - \text{std}(\mathbf{g})),
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{g} = [g_1, \ldots, g_L]$ and $\sigma_\text{min} = 0.05$ is a minimum standard deviation threshold ($\lambda_\text{GAR} = \lambda_\text{div} = 1.0$). This formulation emerged from seven iterations spanning four loss families (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-loss}): cumulative (v1--v2) and maximize (v3--v6) objectives either starve gate gradients at equilibrium or drive gates toward identity; only MSE provides the bidirectional pressure needed to prevent identity collapse.

\subsection{Controlled Comparison Design}\label{sec:comparison-design}

We design a five-way comparison where each adjacent pair isolates one factor: \textbf{ThreadGate (TG) vs.\ Uniform Gate (UG)} (turn conditioning), \textbf{TG vs.\ LoRA-only} (gating mechanism), \textbf{ThreadGate vs.\ +PCGrad} (gradient conflict via gradient surgery \citep{yu2020pcgrad}), and \textbf{Fine-tuned vs.\ Base} (LoRA adaptation). All fine-tuned variants share training data, LoRA configuration, optimizer, and seed (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-hyper}).

